From 2eb6714ce4b3f798dbed1eec41501fb91968cec4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: agents-factory-hubpatch <agents-factory-hubpatch@exe.xyz>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 13:29:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(bd): a contended lock names its holder, with visible
 bounded waits and content-free diagnostics (agents-elu6)

`flock -w 300 <lock> <cmd>` waits in silence and then exits 1 with NO output at all (measured against
util-linux 2.39.3). Measured cost of that on 2026-10-13: a COMPLETE cross-family review could not record
its verdict for more than four minutes because another lane's `bd dolt push` held this VM's shared lock
for the whole network round-trip of its child `git push --force-with-lease refs/dolt/data` - so a lane
holding a finished result produced no output, looked IDLE, and the failure carried no reason. It is the
zombie-lane and silent-spawn failure shape one layer down, and the verdict it delays is the artefact that
authorises a landing.

The holder now names itself: while it holds the lock it writes one record line into the lock FILE (a lock
object, never a data file - flock's lock is on the fd/inode, so writing the record does not disturb it)
and clears it before releasing. A waiter that cannot take the lock reports the holder's lane, role, pid,
operation and how long it has been held, plus the holder's descendant processes - which is where a push
actually spends its minutes.

A DIAGNOSTIC MUST NOT NEED THE CONTENT IT IS DIAGNOSING (agents-zoch). The first version of this patch
stored and reported the holder's full argv, and rev-elu6 defeated it by DRIVING it rather than reading it:
`bd comment issue-x SENTINEL-PRIVATE-PAYLOAD` put that text into the shared lock file AND into the waiting
lane's stderr. Both surfaces belong to other people - the lock file is shared by every lane on the VM and
the report is written into a lane that did not type the text - and bead text can be anything an operator
or an agent typed. So nothing freeform is copied: the operation comes from a fixed vocabulary (anything
else is `unrecognised`), descendant processes are named from a fixed vocabulary (anything else is `other`),
and no bead id, body or argument is stored or printed at all. Fail-closed, so a spelling nobody has thought
of cannot leak either.

- patience is unchanged (300s) and no longer silent: a report on first contention, then at most one per
  FLEET_BD_LOCK_REPORT (default 30s); FLEET_BD_LOCK_WAIT bounds the whole wait
- on expiry it fails FAST with EX_TEMPFAIL (75), the holder named, having changed nothing
- reports go to STDERR: stdout carries bd's JSON for callers such as `bd show --json`
- the lock fd is closed FOR THE bd PAYLOAD ONLY, so a child bd leaves behind cannot keep every lane on the
  VM queued behind it after bd has exited (flock(1)'s child inherits the fd; a shell rewrite loses even
  the attempt unless the fd is closed explicitly)
- an unannotated holder, or a record naming a dead pid, is reported as unnamed/stale rather than as a
  mystery

tests/bd-lock-holder.test.sh pins all of it hermetically: a stub $HOME/.local/bin/bd and a throwaway home,
so it never contends with the fleet. 46 cases, including three that reproduce the reviewer's probe - the
shared record, the waiter's stderr and a descendant's argument vector must each contain no bead text.
Against the pre-change shim the same file reports 23 passed / 23 failed, while the existing
tests/bd-shim.test.sh stays 15/15. Uncontended cost: +0.47ms per call (8.23 -> 8.70ms, best of 6
interleaved 40-call runs). Applies in both orders with the other pending bin/bd patch
(fleet-worktree-beads-guard.patch); the composed shim passes all three suites.

Release-note: none (fleet tooling; no user-facing surface)
